{
  "slug": "artist:ludacris",
  "name": "Ludacris",
  "editorial_article": "Born Christopher Brian Bridges in Champaign, Illinois, Ludacris relocated to Atlanta, Georgia, to begin his career as a radio DJ and intern at Hot 97.5 under the name Chris Lova Lova. He gained early industry attention through a feature on Timbaland's 1998 track 'Phat Rabbit' before launching his independent label, Disturbing tha Peace, in the late 1990s. His 1999 debut 'Incognegro' was re-packaged by Def Jam South as his 2000 major-label arrival 'Back for the First Time,' which reached number four on the Billboard 200. He secured mainstream status with the Kanye West-produced 'Stand Up' in 2003, his first number-one single as a lead artist. His output frequently collaborated with Southern contemporaries like Lil Jon, Shawnna, and Young Jeezy, while his later career included an pivot into voice acting and film, including his long-running role as Tej Parker in the Fast & Furious franchise. He notably received an honorary Bachelor of Science degree in Music Management from Georgia State University in 2022.",
